1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,786
A glass of water.

2
00:00:01,786 --> 00:00:03,122
A spoon of sugar.

3
00:00:03,122 --> 00:00:05,016
A bottle of hand sanitiser.

4
00:00:05,016 --> 00:00:07,379
Each is built from a few elements.

5
00:00:07,779 --> 00:00:10,919
But how much of each one's
mass belongs to each element?

6
00:00:11,369 --> 00:00:14,434
Diya reads a sanitiser
label in her kitchen.

7
00:00:14,434 --> 00:00:19,153
The main ingredient is ethanol,
made of carbon, hydrogen and oxygen.

8
00:00:19,503 --> 00:00:22,201
Which of the three makes
up most of its mass?

9
00:00:22,701 --> 00:00:26,814
By the end, you will split any
compound's mass, element by element.

10
00:00:27,464 --> 00:00:30,335
This split is called
percentage composition.

11
00:00:30,685 --> 00:00:34,749
It tells you what percent of a
compound's mass comes from each element.

12
00:00:35,149 --> 00:00:39,525
Chemists use it to check if a sample
is pure, and to find the formula of

13
00:00:39,525 --> 00:00:40,621
a new compound.

14
00:00:41,221 --> 00:00:45,159
Let's build one ethanol
molecule on our molecule bench.

15
00:00:45,159 --> 00:00:47,359
Two carbon atoms join first.

16
00:00:47,709 --> 00:00:49,543
Oxygen joins one carbon.

17
00:00:49,543 --> 00:00:51,995
Then six hydrogens fill the gaps.

18
00:00:51,995 --> 00:00:55,012
Every bond is a shared pair of electrons.

19
00:00:55,412 --> 00:00:56,882
Now weigh each part.

20
00:00:56,882 --> 00:01:00,533
Two carbons, twenty four
point zero two grams per mole.

21
00:01:00,933 --> 00:01:04,171
Six hydrogens, six point zero four eight.

22
00:01:04,171 --> 00:01:05,928
One oxygen, sixteen.

23
00:01:06,328 --> 00:01:09,930
Together, forty six point
zero six eight grams per mole.

24
00:01:09,930 --> 00:01:12,303
Watch the mass bar split into three.

25
00:01:12,953 --> 00:01:14,658
Now compare two shares.

26
00:01:14,658 --> 00:01:19,096
Hydrogen is six of ethanol's nine
atoms, two thirds of the atoms.

27
00:01:19,496 --> 00:01:22,462
But on the mass bar,
hydrogen's slice is small.

28
00:01:22,862 --> 00:01:24,210
Pause and think.

29
00:01:24,210 --> 00:01:26,744
Is hydrogen most of ethanol's mass?

30
00:01:29,944 --> 00:01:30,844
No!

31
00:01:30,449 --> 00:01:33,328
Hydrogen atoms are the lightest of all.

32
00:01:33,328 --> 00:01:36,614
Six of them weigh less
than one oxygen atom.

33
00:01:37,264 --> 00:01:40,501
So here is the rule, step
by step, using water.

34
00:01:40,851 --> 00:01:41,751
Step one.

35
00:01:41,715 --> 00:01:43,285
Find the molar mass.

36
00:01:43,285 --> 00:01:46,645
Water is eighteen point
zero two grams per mole.

37
00:01:47,395 --> 00:01:48,295
Step two.

38
00:01:48,282 --> 00:01:51,353
Find the mass of the element in one mole.

39
00:01:51,353 --> 00:01:54,241
Oxygen is sixteen point zero zero grams.

40
00:01:54,991 --> 00:01:55,933
Step three.

41
00:01:55,933 --> 00:01:58,044
Divide the part by the whole.

42
00:01:58,044 --> 00:02:00,933
Sixteen divided by
eighteen point zero two.

43
00:02:01,683 --> 00:02:02,632
Step four.

44
00:02:02,632 --> 00:02:04,477
Multiply by one hundred.

45
00:02:04,477 --> 00:02:08,517
Oxygen is eighty eight point
seven nine percent of water.

46
00:02:09,267 --> 00:02:10,312
Remember this.

47
00:02:10,312 --> 00:02:14,485
Percentage composition is a share
of mass, not a count of atoms.

48
00:02:15,185 --> 00:02:17,038
Now, one worked example.

49
00:02:17,038 --> 00:02:18,311
Back to ethanol.

50
00:02:18,611 --> 00:02:23,865
Use carbon twelve point zero one,
hydrogen one point zero zero eight, and

51
00:02:23,865 --> 00:02:25,122
oxygen sixteen.

52
00:02:25,622 --> 00:02:26,634
Molar mass.

53
00:02:26,634 --> 00:02:31,904
Two times twelve point zero one, plus
six times one point zero zero eight,

54
00:02:31,904 --> 00:02:33,026
plus sixteen.

55
00:02:33,026 --> 00:02:35,799
Forty six point zero six eight grams.

56
00:02:36,549 --> 00:02:37,449
Carbon.

57
00:02:37,253 --> 00:02:42,205
Twenty four point zero two over forty
six point zero six eight, times one

58
00:02:42,205 --> 00:02:43,105
hundred.

59
00:02:42,963 --> 00:02:45,375
Fifty two point one four percent.

60
00:02:46,125 --> 00:02:47,025
Hydrogen.

61
00:02:46,941 --> 00:02:51,309
Six point zero four eight over
forty six point zero six eight.

62
00:02:51,309 --> 00:02:53,670
Thirteen point one three percent.

63
00:02:54,420 --> 00:02:55,320
Oxygen.

64
00:02:55,133 --> 00:02:58,298
Sixteen over forty six
point zero six eight.

65
00:02:58,298 --> 00:03:01,009
Thirty four point seven three percent.

66
00:03:01,759 --> 00:03:02,659
Check.

67
00:03:02,372 --> 00:03:05,117
The three add up to one hundred percent.

68
00:03:05,117 --> 00:03:08,174
So carbon is more than
half of ethanol's mass.

69
00:03:08,824 --> 00:03:10,289
Now, the exam trap.

70
00:03:10,639 --> 00:03:12,865
Students take just one carbon.

71
00:03:12,865 --> 00:03:17,567
Twelve point zero one over forty six
point zero six eight gives twenty

72
00:03:17,567 --> 00:03:19,739
six point zero seven percent.

73
00:03:20,139 --> 00:03:22,568
But ethanol has two carbon atoms.

74
00:03:22,568 --> 00:03:25,320
Always multiply by the subscript first.

75
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:30,243
Two times twelve point zero one gives
fifty two point one four percent.

76
00:03:30,893 --> 00:03:32,373
Where is this asked?

77
00:03:32,373 --> 00:03:37,323
As direct mass percent numericals, and
as the first step in finding formulas.

78
00:03:37,923 --> 00:03:39,240
Exam level now.

79
00:03:39,240 --> 00:03:42,399
Find the mass percent of nitrogen in urea.

80
00:03:42,399 --> 00:03:45,981
Its formula is CO, with two NH two groups.

81
00:03:45,981 --> 00:03:49,598
Use C twelve, N fourteen,
O sixteen, H one.

82
00:03:49,598 --> 00:03:50,970
Pause and solve.

83
00:03:56,370 --> 00:03:57,711
The answer is C.

84
00:03:57,711 --> 00:03:59,841
Forty six point seven percent.

85
00:04:00,341 --> 00:04:01,921
Molar mass of urea.

86
00:04:01,921 --> 00:04:07,110
Carbon twelve, oxygen sixteen, and
two NH two groups of sixteen each.

87
00:04:07,110 --> 00:04:08,799
Sixty grams per mole.

88
00:04:09,299 --> 00:04:12,613
Nitrogen is two times
fourteen, twenty eight grams.

89
00:04:12,613 --> 00:04:17,594
Twenty eight over sixty, times one
hundred, is forty six point seven percent.

90
00:04:18,094 --> 00:04:19,815
A used only one nitrogen.

91
00:04:19,815 --> 00:04:23,257
B counted atoms, two out
of eight, instead of mass.

92
00:04:23,857 --> 00:04:25,314
Here is your recap.

93
00:04:25,314 --> 00:04:26,811
Screenshot this card.

94
00:04:30,011 --> 00:04:32,848
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

95
00:04:33,248 --> 00:04:34,148
One.

96
00:04:33,769 --> 00:04:36,537
What does percentage composition tell you?

97
00:04:37,337 --> 00:04:38,237
Two.

98
00:04:37,936 --> 00:04:42,092
Why is hydrogen only thirteen
percent of ethanol's mass?

99
00:04:42,892 --> 00:04:43,792
Three.

100
00:04:43,487 --> 00:04:46,292
Find the mass percent
of hydrogen in water.

101
00:04:47,192 --> 00:04:51,785
Next time: if you know only the
percentages, can you find the formula?

102
00:04:51,785 --> 00:04:54,824
We find out in empirical
and molecular formulae.
